Panama, one of the best um of quality in

00:12:11.519 --> 00:12:13.680
the world. And you will see this

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:16.079
micromanipulators of the microscope. All

00:12:16.079 --> 00:12:18.160
this imaging that I&#x27;m going to show is

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:20.480
happening in a petra dish. Right? So

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:22.000
sometimes it&#x27;s just hard to visualize

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.240
how this is taken. So imagine that all

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:26.160
this is happening here with what we call

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.480
a micromanipulator. All those needles

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:30.399
that you&#x27;ll be seeing in the videos is

00:12:30.399 --> 00:12:33.120
like a jostick. You play with it and

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:35.360
then you have a pedal to move it. But

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:37.200
these are things that sometimes are not

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.760
shown and I just wanted to clarify the

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:43.040
setting of where this magic happens.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:47.120
Now before we start I do want to clarify

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:50.320
two different concepts. One is genetic

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:53.200
selection and one is gene editing which

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:55.920
is the topic of today&#x27;s talk. The reason

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:57.920
why I say it is because sometimes I hear

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:00.480
these terms interchangeable and they are

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:02.880
completely different. Genetic selection

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:04.560
is something that we&#x27;re doing every

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:08.399
single day in IVF clinics that do PGT or

00:13:08.399 --> 00:13:10.959
genetic testing. Now, before we go to

00:13:10.959 --> 00:13:13.920
gene editing, I did want to go over

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:16.240
briefly what genetic testing means.

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:18.160
You&#x27;re going to listen to me saying PGT

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:20.639
a lot. So, PGT would be pre-implantation

00:13:20.639 --> 00:13:22.880
genetic test. That&#x27;s what our company

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:24.959
genomic prediction did. So, in

00:13:24.959 --> 00:13:26.720
pre-implantation genetic testing, of

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:28.880
course, you need the embryo. So, this

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:30.959
embryos comes from the clinic. Again,

00:13:30.959 --> 00:13:33.360
you put the em the egg with the sperm,

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:36.320
fertilize it, let it culture, and then

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:38.079
once you have that blastoise, that

00:13:38.079 --> 00:13:39.200
embryo that I showed you at the

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:40.959
beginning, then you&#x27;re going to take a

00:13:40.959 --> 00:13:43.200
biopsy. I&#x27;m going to show you a video

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:45.440
shortly about how that looks. That

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:47.680
biopsy is sent to the lab. We amplify

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:50.480
the DNA. We interpret the data and then

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:52.800
we just give a PDF to the clinic and the

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:54.959
patient so that they can select which

00:13:54.959 --> 00:13:56.880
embryo would be transferred into the

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:58.160
womb.

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:00.959
Now this is a video of a biopsy again

00:14:00.959 --> 00:14:02.560
just for you guys to see how this

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:05.199
happens. We have this embryo. The shell

00:14:05.199 --> 00:14:07.360
is called zona palucida. Some cells are

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:10.320
coming out. We take that biopsy from

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:11.760
those cells coming out that would

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:14.800
eventually remember become the placenta.

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:16.720
And with a laser it&#x27;s like a pedal. So

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:19.120
it&#x27;s actually like a nice instrument.

00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:21.199
And once you do the laser you kind of

00:14:21.199 --> 00:14:24.000
suck it. Then those cells are put in a

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.800
PCR tube and sent to the laboratories.

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:29.279
What happens to this embryo? It goes to

00:14:29.279 --> 00:14:31.440
liquid nitrogen and it could be there

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:34.000
for years, right? We have pregnancies I

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:36.320
think after 20 years of an embryo being

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:39.440
biopsy and tested. So nothing like we

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:40.880
were talking before about turnaround

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:43.120
times of genetic testing. Liquid

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:46.959
nitrogen that&#x27;s not really an issue.

00:14:46.959 --> 00:14:50.160
Okay. Now that we clarify that concept,

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.480
gene editing is completely different

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:54.240
because in gene editing you&#x27;re actually

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.720
messing with the sequence of DNA. So

00:14:56.720 --> 00:15:00.240
you&#x27;re making changes. Again, genetic

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:02.240
selection, you&#x27;re just screening, seeing

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:04.320
what the embryo already has. With gene

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:06.240
editing, we are actually changing the

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:10.959
DNA of the those cells.

00:15:10.959 --> 00:15:12.880
Now you might have heard the term

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:15.680
CRISPR-Cas9. Um for gene editing,

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:17.360
there are several tools. This is not the

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:19.519
only one. However, this is the one

00:15:19.519 --> 00:15:21.920
that&#x27;s been more developed. Uh there was

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:24.079
a Nobel Prize to two people who

00:15:24.079 --> 00:15:26.320
developed this CRISPR-Cas9. I won&#x27;t

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:28.079
get into detail, but it&#x27;s a system that

00:15:28.079 --> 00:15:31.199
already bacteria has to code against you

00:15:31.199 --> 00:15:34.160
know foreign invasions of virus or other

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:37.680
um um kind of organisms. But basically

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:39.760
the CRISPR-Cas9 became so useful

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:41.440
because once you understand the

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:43.600
components and you get them into the

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:47.040
cell it&#x27;s actually pretty effective in

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:49.759
doing this job. So CRISPR-Cas9 has

00:15:49.759 --> 00:15:52.480
four major components. One component

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:55.519
would be the Cas9. The Cas9 protein

00:15:55.519 --> 00:15:58.880
is this Mickey here. So it&#x27;s an enzyme.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:00.880
It&#x27;s a protein that goes to the DNA and

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:03.600
cuts it. That&#x27;s component number one.

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:06.000
The second component is what we call the

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:08.720
guide RNA. So the guide RNA is a

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:12.560
molecule that uh you can actually design

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:14.320
the sequence whatever you want. You send

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:16.320
there is a company called IDT. So you

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:18.399
send them the sequence what nucleotides

00:16:18.399 --> 00:16:20.560
you want and they will send you a tube

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:23.600
with um some powder which is liophilized

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:25.759
primer. You put some water you have it

00:16:25.759 --> 00:16:28.160
there. It&#x27;s very important because then

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:30.320
you can design whatever sequence you can

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:33.440
target it to any location in the genome.

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:35.680
The third component is of course the

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:38.079
target DNA and in our lecture we&#x27;re

00:16:38.079 --> 00:16:40.880
going to focus on the DNA inside those

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:43.199
embryo cells. And the fourth component

00:16:43.199 --> 00:16:45.600
is something that gets ignored when we

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.199
talk about CRISPR-Cas9 but it&#x27;s

00:16:47.199 --> 00:16:49.920
extremely important and I try to show it

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:53.120
here which is the cell DNA repair

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:55.600
mechanisms because without this CRISPR-Cas9

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:58.399
is just useless. So I&#x27;m just

00:16:58.399 --> 00:17:00.320
going to take it out because for purpose

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:02.880
of showing how this works. So how does

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:04.880
gene editing works? First of all, we

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:06.559
have and I won&#x27;t go in detail into that

00:17:06.559 --> 00:17:09.520
but there is a whole research on how do

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:11.919
we get these components into the cell.

00:17:11.919 --> 00:17:13.280
You could get them of course by

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:16.079
injection which is very easy but it&#x27;s

00:17:16.079 --> 00:17:18.799
very hard to inject you know liquid into

00:17:18.799 --> 00:17:20.720
a single cell which is more than

00:17:20.720 --> 00:17:22.160
microscopic. You could do

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:24.240
electroporation many other techniques

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:25.760
but let&#x27;s just pretend we&#x27;re already

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:28.400
inside the cell. So we design a guide

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:31.840
RNA to a specific sequence and what will

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:35.120
happen is that guide RNA would hybridize

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:37.280
would match to the DNA. So it would kind

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:39.120
of open it. This is happening in the

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:43.679
cell. It puts the whole machinery there

00:17:43.679 --> 00:17:46.480
and the cut happens.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:48.880
It&#x27;s literally a cut. So it generates

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:51.120
what&#x27;s called the double strand DNA

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:54.559
break. And this is not editing. This is

00:17:54.559 --> 00:17:56.720
just cutting the DNA. The editing

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:59.280
happens when the cell DNA repair

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:02.160
mechanisms go and fix this because when

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:05.120
that&#x27;s fixed, it&#x27;s not perfect. You

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:07.520
introduce novel nucleotides

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:09.760
insertions or you can delete

00:18:09.760 --> 00:18:12.080
nucleotides, deletions or you can

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:14.880
actually let&#x27;s say there was a T in that

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:17.440
position the cell could just put an A.

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:19.440
So that is how the gene editing actually

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.520
happen. It&#x27;s not that you are going and

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:24.160
customize and put an A there. you are

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:26.960
relying on the cell repair mechanisms

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:29.360
and therefore as of now it&#x27;s somewhat

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:31.200
random. There are some technologies that

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:33.120
are trying to be more precise but this

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:36.880
is the concept of what happens.

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:38.480
Now

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:40.799
in 2017

00:18:40.799 --> 00:18:43.679
18 there was a major publication of our

00:18:43.679 --> 00:18:46.160
field from the group of Dr. Malib in

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:48.559
Oregon and this was the first proof of

00:18:48.559 --> 00:18:50.960
concept in the human embryo of a

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:53.360
successful gene editing. So basically

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:55.840
what they did they took the egg they

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:58.720
fertilized it with the sperm then they

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:00.880
let it culture one day. This is the

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:03.039
stage of a zygote. You have the two

00:19:03.039 --> 00:19:05.760
genomes mom and dad. And at that stage

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:08.480
they injected the cocktail of proteins

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:10.640
the components that I showed you before.

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:12.240
Now I&#x27;m going to show you a video that

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:14.160
has nothing to do with this paper. It&#x27;s

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:16.640
from our laboratory. But just so you

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:19.280
know how these injections happen. So

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:21.200
basically you have it there. You load

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:24.400
your needle. you put it into the embryo.

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:26.880
That&#x27;s how it&#x27;s happening. Okay? And

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:29.280
then you let the embryo culture, right?

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:31.440
This embryo was cultured until day three

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:33.039
of development when you have around

00:19:33.039 --> 00:19:35.600
eight cells. We call them blastomeres. And

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:38.000
then you analyze them. So the

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:40.720
experimental design was pretty elegant

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:43.039
in this study because what they did was

00:19:43.039 --> 00:19:45.840
to find a sperm from a human who was

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:47.919
already affected with a recessive

00:19:47.919 --> 00:19:51.679
mutation. So all the DNA in that sperm

00:19:51.679 --> 00:19:54.960
had a mutation, right? Therefore the

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:57.760
only outcome in that embryo was to be

00:19:57.760 --> 00:20:00.760
heterosyg

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:03.679
um you know for sure because the sperm

00:20:03.679 --> 00:20:06.000
already had it meaning that if this

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:07.760
actually was working then we would

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:10.480
expect some cells to be fixed or not to

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:12.480
have that mutation.

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:14.160
So this is what they did exactly like

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:17.679
that uh mutant sperm put it in the egg

00:20:17.679 --> 00:20:19.440
and then they got several embryos of

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:22.320
course I don&#x27;t remember how many but one

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:24.720
of the outcomes was what was expected

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:27.280
which is all those cells would have the

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:29.679
mutation so their CRISPR-Cas9 did not

00:20:29.679 --> 00:20:33.200
work but then in some cells they started

00:20:33.200 --> 00:20:37.120
seeing that some cells actually didn&#x27;t

00:20:37.120 --> 00:20:38.960
have the mutation at all and most

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:41.520
importantly few of those embryos not

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:43.200
many because You can see it&#x27;s a mixture

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:45.520
of things. Again, it&#x27;s very random, but

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:48.000
in some of those embryos, all the cells

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:50.400
did not have a mutation. So, you would

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:52.640
understand the impact that this had in

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:54.880
our field because for the first time, we

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:57.840
were fixing or curing a mutation in the

00:20:57.840 --> 00:21:00.240
embryo before it&#x27;s transferred into the

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:02.559
womb.

00:21:02.559 --> 00:21:05.200
The problem here and what happens in

00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:06.640
science and something that I have

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:10.480
learned in my years of this uh field is

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:13.360
that you have to be very careful over

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:17.039
interterpreting data. The analysis that

00:21:17.039 --> 00:21:19.039
was done in those cells, those

00:21:19.039 --> 00:21:21.840
blastomeres, those embryo cells was pretty

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:24.480
shallow analysis. And just because you

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:26.159
didn&#x27;t see the mutation, the assumption

00:21:26.159 --> 00:21:29.360
was that you cured it, right? It was

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:33.280
also in the news and derley which is

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:35.679
Stefan Jarave&#x27;s mentor and with whom we

00:21:35.679 --> 00:21:39.039
collaborate started writing these papers

00:21:39.039 --> 00:21:41.360
trying to provide different

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:44.320
interpretations to the same finding. So

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:46.799
why hold on maybe you are not curing the

00:21:46.799 --> 00:21:48.720
mutation. Maybe you are just not seeing

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:51.520
it. Maybe that chromosome was not even

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:54.640
there. And of course unfortunately in

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:57.440
science there is a lot of egos, right?

00:21:57.440 --> 00:21:59.760
And then you start seeing all these back

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:01.919
and forth back and forth. So even the

00:22:01.919 --> 00:22:05.520
authors of that initial paper they um

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:08.799
brought up this article saying that this

00:22:08.799 --> 00:22:11.039
was yeah it made a lot of sense but was

00:22:11.039 --> 00:22:13.919
sheer speculation and it was true. It

00:22:13.919 --> 00:22:16.400
was just speculation and if you want to

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:18.559
prove a concept you just have to get it

00:22:18.559 --> 00:22:21.520
done. So what we decided to do with

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:23.679
ether and genome prediction was to

00:22:23.679 --> 00:22:25.760
replicate the experiments you know add

00:22:25.760 --> 00:22:28.000
reproducibility because reproducibility

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:29.840
is something that it&#x27;s essential in

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:31.600
science especially when you&#x27;re doing a

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:33.120
biotech company or something like that

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:35.120
you need to have this reproduced in a

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:37.200
different setting by different people

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:39.440
with different interests.

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:42.320
So basically we follow a very similar

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:44.799
experimental design. We took a sperm

00:22:44.799 --> 00:22:48.080
from a guy who was blind. So he had both

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:50.960
of his copies of that gene had the

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:52.960
mutation. They were affected. The gene

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:55.440
was EYS. So the guy was blind. He

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:58.400
donated the sperm. We took donated eggs

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:00.799
and we fertilize those eggs with the

00:23:00.799 --> 00:23:04.159
sperm from the blind um guy. And then

00:23:04.159 --> 00:23:06.480
the experiment was to once we bought the

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:08.720
embryos inject the CRISPR-Cas9

00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:12.720
directed to that EYS gene

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:15.360
make blastocyst. you know the day 5 a.m.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:19.039
development at this point do PGT which

00:23:19.039 --> 00:23:21.679
is taking some cells from the Tactom to

00:23:21.679 --> 00:23:23.360
make sure that the editing happened

00:23:23.360 --> 00:23:25.679
right so that was the first proof of

00:23:25.679 --> 00:23:27.919
concept and another thing we wanted to

00:23:27.919 --> 00:23:30.960
do and we&#x27;re still doing is because this

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:32.400
embryo is not going to get transferred

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.000
you know we can talk about that like in

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:35.440
the future when are we going to transfer

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:37.840
editing embryos but this embryos we took

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:39.919
the inner cell mass which develops into

00:23:39.919 --> 00:23:44.000
the fetus and developed a embryonic

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:46.240
stem cell because when you develop an

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:48.720
embryonic stem cell you don&#x27;t have a

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:50.720
hundred cells to play with you have

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:52.960
hundreds of thousands millions of cells

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:55.200
and this is important because what if

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:57.200
this CRISPR-Cas9 is cutting somewhere

00:23:57.200 --> 00:23:59.919
else we are directing it to cut to one

00:23:59.919 --> 00:24:02.320
gene but it could certainly cut

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:03.919
somewhere else and that&#x27;s what we call

00:24:03.919 --> 00:24:06.159
offtarget effects and until we have

00:24:06.159 --> 00:24:08.960
safety data I do not believe it would be

00:24:08.960 --> 00:24:12.159
a transfer of embryo into a patient so

00:24:12.159 --> 00:24:16.799
anyway Now one thing that was a gamecher

00:24:16.799 --> 00:24:19.600
for this study remember that I told you

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:23.279
before that there was some suspicion

00:24:23.279 --> 00:24:25.039
that there was over interpretation of

00:24:25.039 --> 00:24:27.679
data. So how do you analyze that cell to

00:24:27.679 --> 00:24:29.600
make sure that you are actually not

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:31.120
seeing that mutation and you are not

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:33.679
causing any other harm. There are

00:24:33.679 --> 00:24:38.400
several ways to analyze embry

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:41.279
several technologies. You could do fish,

00:24:41.279 --> 00:24:43.039
you could do something called array

00:24:43.039 --> 00:24:45.360
genome um arbitization, you could do

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:47.840
next generation sequencing. But there is

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:50.640
a specific platform called snip array.

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:52.720
So a snip array is a platform that we

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:55.760
use genomic prediction and it has it

00:24:55.760 --> 00:24:58.799
genotypes up to 800,000 locations in the

00:24:58.799 --> 00:25:01.279
genome. And that these snips are small

00:25:01.279 --> 00:25:03.919
locations that are polymorphic. So maybe

00:25:03.919 --> 00:25:06.320
some of you would have an A alil, I&#x27;ll

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:08.159
have a B al. So they are highly

00:25:08.159 --> 00:25:09.760
polymorphic. In fact, you can actually

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:12.159
predict ancestry based on just these

00:25:12.159 --> 00:25:15.039
locations. So anyway, long story short,

00:25:15.039 --> 00:25:17.919
we had access to this platform and we

00:25:17.919 --> 00:25:19.919
just decided to use it on those cells

00:25:19.919 --> 00:25:22.000
just to make sure whether the editing

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:25.200
was happening correctly or not.

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:27.360
Now before I show you the results, I do

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:29.840
want to walk you through how we read

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:31.360
this data.

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:35.039
So the company that we work at it&#x27;s it

00:25:35.039 --> 00:25:37.600
does PGT. So it&#x27;s a commercial company.

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:39.760
We have we processed last year 40,000

00:25:39.760 --> 00:25:41.840
embryos and this is a small company like

00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:43.360
these are embryos being processed every

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:47.120
year. So the plot that we get is called

00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:50.080
a violin plot. So remember that we have

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:52.640
23 pairs of chromosomes. Well 22 and

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:55.840
then the sexual chromosomes. So here

00:25:55.840 --> 00:25:58.240
each chromosome is represented by one of

00:25:58.240 --> 00:26:00.880
these diamonds. And what this means

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:02.320
basically I don&#x27;t want to go in detail

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:04.880
but as you could see the y-axis has a

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:07.200
little frequency. So if we have two

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:09.360
copies of each chromosome we would

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:12.240
expect the vast majority of snips of

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:14.640
these locations that are hetererozygote

00:26:14.640 --> 00:26:16.559
meaning differing a little from mom and

00:26:16.559 --> 00:26:19.520
dad to be clustered around 50/50 because

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:21.840
we have two copies of the chromosome. So

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:24.799
that&#x27;s why this diamond kind of

00:26:24.799 --> 00:26:27.600
localizes 2.5 because this is confirming

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:29.919
that this embryo has two copies for each

00:26:29.919 --> 00:26:31.919
specific chromosome

00:26:31.919 --> 00:26:34.240
except for chromosome 16. Again it&#x27;s

00:26:34.240 --> 00:26:36.720
just an example for chromosome 16. You

00:26:36.720 --> 00:26:38.000
can see that there is no data in the

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:40.240
middle and what this suggests is

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:42.320
something in genetics that we call loss

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:44.240
of heterosygosity.

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:47.440
That is because there&#x27;s only one copy of

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:50.400
chromosome 16 and this is therefore an

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:53.200
abnormal embryo. In fact, we have so

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:55.679
much resolution um per embryo. Remember

00:26:55.679 --> 00:26:57.360
this is coming from just six cells of

00:26:57.360 --> 00:26:59.760
that are being tested. You can visualize

00:26:59.760 --> 00:27:02.320
the entire chromosome short arm and long

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:04.159
arm and you can see that there is no

00:27:04.159 --> 00:27:06.159
data in the middle. So this is how we do

00:27:06.159 --> 00:27:08.480
confirmations that there is a loss of a

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:10.559
chromosome. And of course the report of

00:27:10.559 --> 00:27:12.559
that patient would say you have an

00:27:12.559 --> 00:27:14.240
abnormal embryo with a loss of

00:27:14.240 --> 00:27:16.880
chromosome 16. Don&#x27;t even waste money

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:18.880
and time transferring it because it will

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:21.440
result into an abortion or you know a

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:24.320
biochemical pregnancy. Anyway, the

00:27:24.320 --> 00:27:25.919
reason why I show you all this is

00:27:25.919 --> 00:27:28.799
because this tool was not used before in

00:27:28.799 --> 00:27:30.480
the previous research and as you could

00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:32.799
see it gives you a lot of information on

00:27:32.799 --> 00:27:35.200
these cells.

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:36.480
So now I&#x27;m going to show you the

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:38.400
results. So just to recap the

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:42.159
experimental design, we took the egg um

00:27:42.159 --> 00:27:44.240
that was donated, the sperm from the guy

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:47.120
who was blind, fertilized the embryo,

00:27:47.120 --> 00:27:49.840
injected the CRISPR-Cas9 cocktail,

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:52.480
developed the embryo until day three and

00:27:52.480 --> 00:27:55.120
then until day five. We took the biopsy

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:58.320
for PGT and tested. Remember that that

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:00.720
that gene that we had for this specific

00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:03.840
uh experiment was in chromosome 6. The

00:28:03.840 --> 00:28:07.200
gene was called EYS and is located here

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:09.039
close to the centromere which is that

00:28:09.039 --> 00:28:11.600
part that unites both short and long arm

00:28:11.600 --> 00:28:14.399
of the chromosome.

00:28:14.399 --> 00:28:16.480
And this is one example that came in the

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:18.799
paper. So as you could see this is for

00:28:18.799 --> 00:28:21.520
those who work in the IVF industry. This

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:23.200
is a very beautiful embryo. It&#x27;s very

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:25.200
good morphology. So that was good

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:26.799
because we were not messing up with the

00:28:26.799 --> 00:28:29.760
morphology of embryo development.

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:31.840
And once we visualize all the

00:28:31.840 --> 00:28:32.960
chromosomes, but this is just the

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:35.600
example of chromosome 6, short arm, long

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:38.159
arm, we started seeing something that

00:28:38.159 --> 00:28:41.039
was unexpected. If you look at the short

00:28:41.039 --> 00:28:44.240
arm, remember what I showed you before

00:28:44.240 --> 00:28:47.600
here, you see data around 0.5, which is

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:49.600
expected because you expect two copies

00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:52.240
of each chromosome. But what was very

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:55.039
unusual is that right at the point of

00:28:55.039 --> 00:28:58.559
cut of CRISPR-Cas9 we started seeing

00:28:58.559 --> 00:29:01.039
loss of heterozygosity.

00:29:01.039 --> 00:29:02.720
We thought at the beginning that it was

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:05.760
a denovo um what we call deletion

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:07.200
because this means that you only have

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:10.080
one copy of that part of the chromosome

00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:11.760
because it happens every every day like

00:29:11.760 --> 00:29:13.360
you see the noble duplications or

00:29:13.360 --> 00:29:16.559
deletions but it kept happening and

00:29:16.559 --> 00:29:19.279
always right at the point of cut that we

00:29:19.279 --> 00:29:22.000
designed the CRISPR-Cas9 to go in

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:25.279
other embryos. The finding was even more

00:29:25.279 --> 00:29:28.399
striking. We were seeing loss of

00:29:28.399 --> 00:29:30.399
heterozygosity everywhere in the

00:29:30.399 --> 00:29:33.840
chromosome. And after doing so much data

00:29:33.840 --> 00:29:36.720
and data and seeing the same thing, we

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:40.799
concluded that CRISPR-Cas9 unlike

00:29:40.799 --> 00:29:42.559
what they interpreted in the previous

00:29:42.559 --> 00:29:44.240
paper, it&#x27;s not that it was getting rid

00:29:44.240 --> 00:29:46.080
of the mutation. It was getting rid of

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:48.080
the entire chromosome or part of the

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:52.559
chromosome. So that&#x27;s why CRISPR-Cas9

00:29:52.559 --> 00:29:55.600
was actually not fixing the mutation. it

00:29:55.600 --> 00:29:58.880
was eliminating the entire chromosome or

00:29:58.880 --> 00:30:00.799
part of it.

00:30:00.799 --> 00:30:03.200
So you can imagine the implications of

00:30:03.200 --> 00:30:05.200
this. You know this was an unexpected

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:06.720
finding.

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:08.799
We presented in several conferences.

00:30:08.799 --> 00:30:10.399
There was a lot of I think I have a

00:30:10.399 --> 00:30:11.760
couple. Well, this is the paper that

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:14.399
came out from Michael Zukado. It

00:30:14.399 --> 00:30:16.000
actually helped us to understand a lot

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:18.320
of cell repair mechanisms in the embryo.

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:20.640
But the major finding was that CRISPR-Cas9

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:22.799
was not doing what it was

00:30:22.799 --> 00:30:24.960
intended to do. it was actually

00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:28.399
eliminating DNA.

00:30:28.399 --> 00:30:30.240
Now the cool thing again I always

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:32.559
emphasize on reproducibility after some

00:30:32.559 --> 00:30:34.559
groups saw these findings they

00:30:34.559 --> 00:30:36.320
replicated the Francis Creek Institute

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:38.240
in London which is very has a lot of

00:30:38.240 --> 00:30:41.679
prestige um also in the European meeting

00:30:41.679 --> 00:30:43.760
there is also other groups using this

00:30:43.760 --> 00:30:46.240
nipper and they&#x27;re finding exactly the

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:48.000
same.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:50.640
Now again you would expect

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:53.360
every when things go okay people are

00:30:53.360 --> 00:30:55.760
like okay when things go wrong everyone

00:30:55.760 --> 00:30:58.159
is on India right so if you could see

00:30:58.159 --> 00:31:01.360
all these uh titles of all these major

00:31:01.360 --> 00:31:05.120
um newspapers CRISPR is bad CRISPR is

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:07.760
not ready CRISPR will never be the

00:31:07.760 --> 00:31:11.039
thing the solution for gene editing and

00:31:11.039 --> 00:31:12.880
it&#x27;s true because we found something

00:31:12.880 --> 00:31:14.559
that wasn&#x27;t expected and remember we

00:31:14.559 --> 00:31:16.799
need safety data before we do this in

00:31:16.799 --> 00:31:18.399
the embryo

00:31:18.399 --> 00:31:19.919
But

00:31:19.919 --> 00:31:21.360
there is something in the field of

00:31:21.360 --> 00:31:23.840
infertility that we have to cope every

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:26.320
single day and that is embryonic

00:31:26.320 --> 00:31:28.799
aneuploidy.

00:31:28.799 --> 00:31:31.679
So embryonic aneuploidy first of all is the

00:31:31.679 --> 00:31:33.279
most common genetic abnormality in

00:31:33.279 --> 00:31:35.840
humans. What does aneuploidy mean? Remember

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:38.559
that we we as humans we have 46

00:31:38.559 --> 00:31:41.120
chromosomes 22 pairs plus a sex

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:42.720
chromosome.

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:45.840
If a human or an embryo has more

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:48.399
chromosomes than 46 or less than 46,

00:31:48.399 --> 00:31:51.120
that&#x27;s considered an anuplo.

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:53.440
And what&#x27;s even worse, at least for our

00:31:53.440 --> 00:31:56.399
field, is that aneuploidy is the most

00:31:56.399 --> 00:31:58.480
common genetic abnormality of

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:01.279
infertility. So this is a plot that we

00:32:01.279 --> 00:32:03.120
did when I was training at RM of New

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:06.080
Jersey, a huge IVF program in Jersey. So

00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:08.640
we took patients from all maternal age

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:11.679
and as you could see the older the woman

00:32:11.679 --> 00:32:14.960
gets or the egg gets the higher the rate

00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:17.279
of embryonic aneuploidy because

00:32:17.279 --> 00:32:19.120
remember that women are born with all

00:32:19.120 --> 00:32:20.640
the eggs that are going to be produced

00:32:20.640 --> 00:32:23.039
during her lifetime. Right? So a woman

00:32:23.039 --> 00:32:26.080
who is 40 or 45 years old has eggs that

00:32:26.080 --> 00:32:28.320
have been there since she was born. So

00:32:28.320 --> 00:32:31.120
the aging of X results in having embryos

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:33.279
with annuploites

00:32:33.279 --> 00:32:36.640
more extra or less chromosomes. But the

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:38.480
reason why I bring this here is because

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:40.799
for infertility this is a huge issue.

00:32:40.799 --> 00:32:43.120
That&#x27;s why infertility clinics we get a

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:45.440
lot of older women because they try and

00:32:45.440 --> 00:32:47.440
try and try and reason why they don&#x27;t

00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:49.360
get pregnant most of the cases is

00:32:49.360 --> 00:32:51.120
because their embryos have an extra

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:54.159
chromosome for example. So it was

00:32:54.159 --> 00:32:56.640
actually sitting down and be okay we

00:32:56.640 --> 00:32:59.600
have potentially a solution to eliminate

00:32:59.600 --> 00:33:02.480
chromosomes which are already ahead for

00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:05.760
the infertility clinics and that was um

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:08.320
something that we started doing. So

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:10.480
again I won&#x27;t get into detail but this

00:33:10.480 --> 00:33:13.279
was the first time that we could find a

00:33:13.279 --> 00:33:15.760
tool that could get rid of embryonic

00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:18.240
aneuploidy. So cure tricoms for example

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:20.480
cure down syndrome from the um

00:33:20.480 --> 00:33:22.880
pre-implantation embryo. So the

00:33:22.880 --> 00:33:25.440
experimental design basically it was to

00:33:25.440 --> 00:33:28.320
take the egg remember that an egg is

00:33:28.320 --> 00:33:29.519
produced through a process called

00:33:29.519 --> 00:33:32.320
gamtogenesis and then you have a stem

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:35.519
cell that divides into three two cells

00:33:35.519 --> 00:33:37.840
and then these two cells into other two

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:40.080
and there are two cells that are a

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:42.320
byproduct of this division which are

00:33:42.320 --> 00:33:44.559
called the polar bodies right and the

00:33:44.559 --> 00:33:47.440
polar bodies are these small cells that

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:49.919
get DNA because it&#x27;s a division so they

00:33:49.919 --> 00:33:51.919
get equal part of DNA but they just

00:33:51.919 --> 00:33:54.399
don&#x27;t get the cytoplasm and the rest of

00:33:54.399 --> 00:33:57.679
chemicals only one cell becomes the egg.

00:33:57.679 --> 00:33:59.200
But the reason why it becomes more

00:33:59.200 --> 00:34:01.840
important is if we can actually analyze

00:34:01.840 --> 00:34:04.320
the polar body and let&#x27;s say one polar

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:06.880
body has an extra chromosome 16, we can

00:34:06.880 --> 00:34:08.399
assume that the egg has the missing

00:34:08.399 --> 00:34:11.440
chromosome 16 or vice versa. If the

00:34:11.440 --> 00:34:14.320
polar body has a missing chromosome 16,

00:34:14.320 --> 00:34:16.320
we can assume that the egg has the extra

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:18.720
chromosome 16. So the experimental

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:21.760
design was to do this and then if we

00:34:21.760 --> 00:34:23.440
find that the polar body has a missing

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:25.760
chromosome 16 why don&#x27;t we inject

00:34:25.760 --> 00:34:28.079
CRISPR-Cas9 directed to that

00:34:28.079 --> 00:34:31.200
chromosome 16 so we can get rid of it

00:34:31.200 --> 00:34:32.960
and this was actually performed here in

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:36.000
Columbia University so Jenna Turosi who

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:37.919
was the fellow by then she started doing

00:34:37.919 --> 00:34:40.480
these experiments again you will analyze

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:43.119
the polar body actually I show you the

00:34:43.119 --> 00:34:45.599
biopsy of the embryo but back in the day

00:34:45.599 --> 00:34:47.520
before we didn&#x27;t know whether we could

00:34:47.520 --> 00:34:49.280
touch the embryo. We were doing polar

00:34:49.280 --> 00:34:51.599
body biopsies, right? That was the old

00:34:51.599 --> 00:34:53.200
standard before. Now, no one does it

00:34:53.200 --> 00:34:54.800
because the egg is extremely delicate

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:57.520
and most of them die. But in this case,

00:34:57.520 --> 00:34:58.800
we managed I mean this were for

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:01.920
research. These are all human eggs. We

00:35:01.920 --> 00:35:05.440
took the polar body, we analyzed it and

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:08.240
some of those polar bodies, at least for

00:35:08.240 --> 00:35:12.000
this egg, one has amazing chromosome 13,

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:13.680
but the other one was very useful

00:35:13.680 --> 00:35:15.520
because it didn&#x27;t have any signal of

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:17.599
chromosome 16. So we could assume that

00:35:17.599 --> 00:35:19.520
the extra chromosome was in the egg and

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:21.839
if that egg was going to get fertilized

00:35:21.839 --> 00:35:23.440
that will be an embryo with three copies

00:35:23.440 --> 00:35:26.400
of chromosome 16 the two maternal and

00:35:26.400 --> 00:35:31.599
the paternal one. So after we did that

00:35:31.599 --> 00:35:34.240
we designed the guide RNA remember this

00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:36.160
is the part of the CRISPR-Cas9 you

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:38.880
can design directed to the chromosome 16

00:35:38.880 --> 00:35:40.880
from the mom because you didn&#x27;t want to

00:35:40.880 --> 00:35:42.720
get rid of the paternal chromosome 16.

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:44.640
It&#x27;s important that you have the two

00:35:44.640 --> 00:35:46.960
copies of each chromosome, one from mom,

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:49.200
one from dad. Not in all, but in most

00:35:49.200 --> 00:35:51.760
chromosomes. So anyway, what happened is

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:54.720
that we did this injection. We cultured

00:35:54.720 --> 00:35:56.960
this embryo until day three. We took

00:35:56.960 --> 00:36:00.400
each cell and analyzed it. And we had we

00:36:00.400 --> 00:36:03.280
got actually a myriad of results. Some

00:36:03.280 --> 00:36:05.040
of them had three copies of chromosome

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:07.440
16. So we confirmed that our assumption

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:09.119
was correct. Some cell had that

00:36:09.119 --> 00:36:10.720
exchromosome.

